WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.142
A self join means joining a table to itself.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:08.912
We use two aliases so the same physical table can play two logical roles.

00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:10.962
Look at the small staff table.

00:00:10.962 --> 00:00:15.757
Ana and Ben are both in department A, while Cara is in department B.

00:00:15.757 --> 00:00:20.972
We treat one copy of the table as s1 and another copy as s2.

00:00:20.972 --> 00:00:23.962
Then we match rows when the department is the same.

00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:28.957
If we stopped there, we would get unwanted pairs such as Ana paired with herself,

00:00:28.957 --> 00:00:33.672
Ben paired with himself, and the same colleague pair in both directions.

00:00:33.672 --> 00:00:40.532
The condition that the first staff ID must be less than the second staff ID solves both problems.

00:00:40.532 --> 00:00:45.977
It prevents self-pairs and keeps only one direction for each pair.

00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:51.152
In the result, department A produces one unique colleague pair: Ana and Ben.

00:00:51.152 --> 00:00:57.522
Self joins are useful whenever rows in one table must be compared with other rows from that same

00:00:57.522 --> 00:01:03.662
table. Examples include employee-manager relationships, comparing earlier and later events,

00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:09.681
matching products in the same category, or identifying pairs of staff members who share a department.
